CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20 --unwind 6 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ invoke via pack-expansion decltype trait counts 1: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
[temp.deduct.call]/1 + [temp.variadic]/5: a class template member typedef
computed as decltype(declval<F>()(declval<A>()...)) -- a decltype whose call
expression contains a pack expansion -- is used as the return type of the
function template `invoke`, which is itself called with a *deduced* parameter
pack A from inside another template (count_matches).

Root cause (pack-expansion rework phase 3 / phase 5 mechanism): when
guess_function_template_args deduced `invoke`'s parameter pack A, it recorded A
only as a scalar binding in template_map.type_map and left pack_args_map /
pack_size_map empty.  Elaborating the dependent return type
invoke_result<F, A...>::type then substituted the single deduced type for `A`
but left a dangling `...` expansion marker on it; the subsequent attempt to
pack-expand a non-pack type failed, the failure was swallowed by the deduction's
SFINAE guard, and the caller's body was silently discarded (returning nondet).

Fixed by recording the deduced function parameter pack in pack_args_map /
pack_size_map (in addition to type_map) before the return type is elaborated, so
the pack-expansion machinery (gated on pack_args_map) expands declval<A>()... and
Trait<F, A...> per element and drops the `...` marker.  Part of the unified
pack-expansion rework documented in
doc/architectural/cpp-variadic-pack-expansion-rework.md.
